  "text_md": "MS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 141.\nPERTY. Pait. Lydgate.\n; the treat, green field peas boiled in the shells. Yorksh.\n![](<11_pg146_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\nAs for his sallets, better never was Then acute sorrell, and sweet three-leav'd grasse, And for a sawee he seldome is at charges. For every crab-tree doth aflfbord him vergis ;\nThat may aftur be pertenere\nTaijor's Workes, 1630, i. 97-\nPESTERMENT. Embarrassment. North.\n(2) To sooth ; to appease. Tylle y be sewre of youre hartys ese. Nothing but hit may my grevys pese.\nPERUR. A kind of cup. PERUSE. To examine, or survey.\nPERYSSE. Pears. {A.-N.)\nDayly he propherid batayle : the enmys durst not fyghte\nPESSIPE. A kind of cup.\nMS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 47.\nPERSPICIL. An optic-glass. It occurs in\nThen was the tre ful of ripe perysse, And began down to falle.\nPERSUADERS. Spurs. Also, pistols.\nPESON. An instrument in the form of a staff, with balls or crockets, used for weighing be-\n;\nready\nand that the mariners and souldiers would not yeeld to goe the voyage till they were paid their\narrears. MS. HarU 383.\nThan lay sche uncovert\n![](<11_pg146_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\nPESTERED. Crowded. Peele, ii. 235.\nPERTRYCHE. A partridge.\nMS. Bibl. Reg. 17 D. xv.\nOflFhevon blisse God graunte tham perty,\n•\nMorte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 84.\nPESE. (1) Peace. Perceval 980, 981.\nIllustrations of Fairy Mythology, p. 11. PERTE. (1) To part. Still in use.\nPresez to his penowne, and per«i/ it hentes.\nPERTENERE. A partner.\nMS. Cantab. Ff. i. G. f. 132.\nMany men spekes of lamentacioun, OfTmoders and of their gret desolatioun.\n;\nto the helmet. \" A pesane and a paunsone,\"\nThe terys ran out of his een gray\n!\nPESTLE. (1) A leg of an animal, generally of a pig. A pestle of pork is still in common use. \" Pestels of venison,\" Warner's Antiq. Culin. p. 98. *• Pestell of flesshe,^'amJon,\" Palsgrave.\n(2) Of good appearance. Ther was no man in the kynges lande More perte then was he.\nRyght as the pertryche is constreyned undir the claues and nayles of the hauke, is as halfe deed for drede. Caxton's Divers Fruyt/iil Ghostly Maters.\nA pestle-pie is a large standing pie which contains a whole gammon, and sometimes a couple of fowls and a neat's tongue, a favorite dish at country fairs, and at Christmas feasts.\nHis banket sometimes is greene beanes and peason. Nuts, peares, plumbes, apples, as they are in season.\nmaking in summer evenings\nOf hevene, whan we hens schall goo.\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 244.\nWhich that thay did indure\nmyjte\nMS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 114.",
